the investigation at the blast site has  started in earnest  three days after the explosion the  cordoned off area near where the  ammonium nitrate was stored  is being scoured for clues or evidence  left behind  in this field of debris among the  investigators  bomb disposal specialists experienced in  identifying  and dealing with explosives the harbour  is also being searched  with teams of divers kitted out in scuba  diving equipment  making repeated trips the lebanese  government has given an investigative  committee  four days to determine responsibility  for this tragedy  but amidst the investigation there's  also an increasingly desperate search  for survivors  they've already located what appears to  be the start of an underground network  of panic rooms  built beneath the giant silo stores now  they're trying to shift the piles of  rubble and spilt grain laying on top  so they can investigate  they know that there's a labyrinth of  subterranean chambers here  they've discovered the opening of just  one of them  and if you come a bit closer you can see  the top of  what looks like the ceiling of one  and that's what is keeping the hopes of  relatives alive  that somehow their loved ones have made  it  to that shelter a turkish group with  experience in rescuing people after  earthquakes  isn't giving up yet we encountered like  these situations before  when you've actually pulled people out  after this length of time  yes so you're still holding on to hope  every time yeah social rescue is  every time have has hopes  human life is very important human life  is  over everything therefore every time we  have  thoughts but it's an agonizing weight  for the relatives of those still missing  who don't know whether they're alive or  dead  as rooty is head of the operations room  for the port silos  his wife and youngest daughter are keen  to show us photos of him  he told them never to worry about his  safety at the port  when the civil war was happening they  used to go  there and when there was explosions  blasts and everything they would hide  down there  so she thinks he knows how to handle  this  but his family and friends are furious  at the lack of government action  on all fronts 40 hours  it has been 40 hours before the  equipment and the machinery  went to the site where is my father so  we have hopes we still have hopes even  though it's getting diminished  by every second that they are late  and they are  they have no faith in the promises of a  government inquiry  and they're determined someone is held  to account for this  i'm gonna translate this anger into  something more effective and work harder  harder  each and every time to get justice  not just for my dad not just for the  missing people  but for all lebanon  so many families have been torn apart by  this tragedy  and so many feel utterly let down by  those in charge  alex crawford sky news beirut
 

